WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.350
- In today's video, I'm going
to talk about presentations.

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.320
But not how to do and conduct them,

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.950
but rather how to
properly prepare for them.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.970
And the inspiration for today's video

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.250
was with the conversation
that I recently had

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.060
with another former McKinsey colleague.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.010
This person has a leadership position

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:17.890
in a big industry company.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:19.267
And this person was telling me,

00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:21.490
"Heinrich, at my current company,

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.820
there are always these
presentations going on.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.370
They almost always start late."

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:27.590
And the main reason for that

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:30.410
is that people do not prepare properly

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:31.460
for this presentation.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:32.500
And this is a problem.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.250
And this is surely also an area

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.090
where I've seen many consultants
with significant mistakes,

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:39.570
which then were perceived
in a very negative way,

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:43.010
both by clients, but often
also your internal leadership,

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.890
your partners, or maybe
part of the meeting as well.

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:47.750
So welcome to another coffee break

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.850
here on my channel, Firm Learning.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.500
My name is Heinrich, and on
my channel I want to help you

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.020
to become successful in the
first years of your career.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.170
So without further ado, let's get into it.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.670
I prepared a slide with
a couple of key points

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.170
that I will now present to you.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:03.220
Let's get started.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.620
So now I did prepare a
couple of points for you

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:08.330
on this topic of the right preparation.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.290
And the first point is
really your mindset.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.500
How are you thinking about that?

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.390
And here I wanna share two
things, two essential points.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:20.230
And the first is that you
should make this your problem.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:21.880
I've seen it so many times

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.260
that there were business
analysts, junior conservatives,

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.360
maybe interns as part of the team.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.620
And then they left it to the project lead

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.600
to figure all these things out.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.600
This is clearly not how it should be.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.660
Now, this is not supposed
to sound too hierarchical,

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.480
but at least to me it seems obvious

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.800
that you as the junior
person should make sure

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:43.000
that you make these
things your responsibility

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.020
to keep this weight off the shoulders

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.780
of the more senior person in your team.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.110
Very much related to this

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:51.300
is then the behavior in the
meeting room, in the meeting.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.790
If you actually find out

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.800
that something is not working as planned,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.280
not working as expected, then here again,

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.320
I've observed it so many times
that there were associates,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.610
junior people, interns in the room,

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.120
but then they left it up to the partner

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.240
or up to the project lead
to fix certain things

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.930
with the technical devices.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.980
This will surely not be perceived well.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.210
If you see that anything
isn't working properly,

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.440
you should jump.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.760
You should take responsibility

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.330
and make sure that it all works.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:21.240
Also for the reason

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.430
that in the room there
will often be some clients,

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.690
some other guests.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:26.220
Then it would be great for the partner

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.580
to be able to entertain these people,

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.690
have a cordial conversation with them,

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.100
instead of them needing to fix

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.020
all the technical devices in the room.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.930
Next is preparation of the room.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:38.570
And here I trust, we all
already had our experiences

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:40.250
of being in a meeting room,

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.110
maybe it was quite a big room

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:44.390
with many, many people present,

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.930
but there was only one small microphone

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:48.250
in the middle of the room.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:50.730
And then people were not
really able to participate.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:51.960
There was a person sitting

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.880
at the other end of the
room saying something,

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.870
but this person was not
being heard in the room

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.460
because just the microphone configuration

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:01.620
did not allow that.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.270
And this of course is not great.

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.510
Especially if you are the
host of such a meeting,

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.080
you're inviting a
significant number of people,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.510
always make sure that the room setup

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:13.140
fits the audience that
you're inviting there.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.670
And if you're even somewhere else,

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.270
maybe at a building of a client,

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.970
you're booking a room and you're out there

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.930
and realize it doesn't
work, then look out.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:21.820
Maybe you'll find a second microphone,

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:23.340
a second microphone device.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.000
Maybe talk with the assistant

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.950
of the sponsoring board member.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.240
The assistant might be able to help you.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:30.930
You might be able to get some devices

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:32.730
out of one of the other meeting rooms

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.360
maybe that are close by.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.820
Be there early, look at the setup,

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:38.430
and make sure that the setup

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.890
actually suits the needs of your audience.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.580
Related to this point of preparation

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:45.400
is to make sure that you
are dialed in on time.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.700
And again, I've seen it so many times.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.520
You were there shortly before the meeting.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.120
Not everyone is there.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.280
Now it's exactly on time to start it.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.880
And then you realize, "Oh,
we are not even dialed in."

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.060
We are not even dialed in
to the video conference yet.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:01.310
We're not even dialed in to
the telephone conference yet.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:03.140
Or maybe it was not even clear

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:06.020
whether anybody was
dialing in from outside,

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:08.910
whether all people are sitting
there in the meeting room.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:11.530
Of course in these
situations, your partner,

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:14.290
your project lead will likely look at you

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:16.720
as the junior person, as the associate,

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.570
the expectation will be there

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.570
that you will have organized all this.

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:23.160
That you will have this
all top of your mind,

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.090
what you need to do to get
everybody in, everybody involved.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.300
And then the last point
is organizing flipcharts,

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.950
whiteboards, and similar
pieces of equipment.

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:32.870
So if you're working
for a consulting firm,

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:34.360
for you it's maybe standard

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.640
that in every room these
things are present.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.040
But trust me that in many
client organizations,

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.300
this is not the case.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.110
So ask yourself what you really need,

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:43.760
what will your meeting rely on?

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.840
And if you realize that
you need these things,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.380
either bring them yourself
if the setup allows,

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.630
or make sure that via
the personal assistant,

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:52.040
whomever you have at your disposal

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.640
to help you with these things
in the client organization,

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:56.130
that you reach out to them and make sure

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:59.050
that it's all set up once you're
there and once you need it.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:00.260
Next, a couple of points

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:02.540
on what you should probably
always carry with you

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:05.870
as a way a prepared associate,
as a young professional

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.260
who is setting up and
conducting meetings like this.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.930
First is adapters for
beamers and monitors.

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:12.930
So nowadays, all people are walking around

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:13.930
with their MacBooks.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:15.630
You will frequently find yourself in rooms

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:18.340
where there's maybe a beamer
with an old VGA output,

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:20.930
an HDMI cable, any other
of these standards,

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.440
and all of (indistinct)
the right interfaces

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.400
for your device.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.920
Make sure to always
carry a bunch of adapters

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.200
in order to be able to connect

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.710
all kinds of different
beamers and monitors,

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:34.240
even if the technology is rather outdated.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:35.740
Next, the other thing is a presenter.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.320
So something like this.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.820
This is also something
I always have in my bag.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:41.190
It's a nice and easy device.

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:42.900
You can plug it into every PC,

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.830
and then you don't have
to do this little thing

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:46.920
of somebody may be clicking,

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:48.700
advancing the slides on the PC,

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.830
and then someone else
presenting something,

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:52.570
and then this is not really in sync.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.990
Maybe then someone is already
advancing to the next slide

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:55.911
where the presenter

00:05:55.911 --> 00:05:57.860
is still wanting to talk
about the last slide.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.510
Ah, this is always very cumbersome.

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:01.920
It's so great if you could
just always carry a presenter,

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:02.960
plug it in the device,

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:05.590
give the presenter to your
partner or to your project lead,

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:07.000
whoever is presenting.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.610
And again, this will make
it look as quite organized,

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:12.240
quite well organized if you
always carry these things,

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:13.073
have them with you.

00:06:13.073 --> 00:06:14.550
And then at the right moment in time,

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.240
you can just drag them out, plug them in,

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:17.540
and it just all works.

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:19.540
Next is always carry with you

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.390
some additional spare
audio conferencing devices.

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:24.500
So as a consultant, I was
always carrying with me

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:27.170
a set of two little audio spiders.

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:28.870
So these combination devices

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:31.140
where you have both a
microphone and a speaker.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:34.110
Because with these you can
easily plug them into your PC.

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:37.210
And then two of them, you can
often combine them, sync them.

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:39.850
And then with two of them,
even in a larger meeting room,

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:42.470
this usually always gave
you enough flexibility

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:45.440
to provide full audio
and microphone coverage

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.290
of a larger meeting room.

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:50.100
So here are different brands
offering solutions like that.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.610
I very frequently use the Jabra devices

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:54.130
which I also own myself.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:55.750
I will link now in the video description

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:58.260
some links to these devices
that I personally use.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.460
So if you're interested in
that, just check them out.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:02.580
And I also wanna share
some points of advice

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:05.000
on online meetings, online video meetings.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.790
Because of course I very well understand

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:08.250
that many of these physical meetings

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:10.030
are not taking place at the moment.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:11.390
And here maybe some obvious things.

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:13.430
Of course, make sure that
you're sitting in an area

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.167
where you have a stable
internet connection.

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:18.200
And of course, also not too
much noise surrounding you.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:19.460
And this might sound obvious,

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.680
but I still very, very vividly
remember the conversation

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:23.600
of two partners.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.340
And one of my prior employers
where it was not his customer,

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.560
the senior consultant should
be promoted to project lead.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:32.440
And one partner made a
very hard case against that

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:35.720
because this partner had an
experience with this consultant,

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:37.470
but this consultant was conducting

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:40.390
a probably rather important
client conversation

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:41.650
from a train.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:43.900
He was in an audio
conference from a train.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:46.740
Due to the of course bad
telephone connection in trains,

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.920
he was dropping in and off
of the call all the time.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.930
And this partner was making the point

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:53.400
that apparently this consultant

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:56.260
is not taking the
responsibility seriously enough,

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:58.280
that this consultant should have made sure

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.070
that when he is on an
important client call,

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:03.160
that he is not sitting in a train.

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:05.510
That he is making sure that
he has a good connection,

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:08.480
that he can conduct these
calls in a professional manner

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:10.670
as the client will expect
this consultant to.

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:11.870
So frankly, when I heard that,

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:13.290
I thought that this was quite tough,

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:15.320
that only for one incident like that,

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.020
your promotion is questioned.

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:19.210
But apparently, obviously, some people,

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:20.600
things like that to some people,

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:22.040
this is extremely important.

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:24.440
So make sure you have these
things in mind as well,

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.540
to just not endanger your next promotion,

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:28.340
to not endanger your career as well.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:30.670
Another point on online video conference

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:33.060
is of course the proper technical setup.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:35.010
Most people nowadays have laptops

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:36.687
where there's an integrated microphone

00:08:36.687 --> 00:08:38.420
and an integrated camera.

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:40.620
And most video conferences
are just conducted

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:42.320
by sitting in front of a laptop,

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:43.717
using exactly these devices.

00:08:43.717 --> 00:08:45.370
And I trust this will be the case

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:48.950
for most regular interactions,
most regular conferences.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:51.810
But especially if you're having
some very important meetings

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:53.130
with very senior people,

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:54.580
consider whether you don't want

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.440
to upgrade your equipment a bit.

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:58.070
And here now in my current role,

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.210
I had the pleasure to do
the last month's interview.

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:01.860
Lots of people, lots of candidates.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.130
And you would be surprised

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:06.860
how many of these people were
sitting in dark environments.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:08.337
You could barely properly see them

00:09:08.337 --> 00:09:11.990
due to the low quality camera
that they used in the laptop.

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:14.140
Maybe there were also noise interferences.

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:15.910
The audio was not very clear.

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:18.810
Of course this will not help
you to make a great impression.

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:19.650
So think about that.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:22.390
Don't you have one,
two, 300 euro to spare,

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:25.600
to invest in a proper microphone
and some basic lights?

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.520
Maybe even an external
camera to properly make sure

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:30.080
that you have a nice picture.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:31.910
With a couple of hundred
bucks, you will be able

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:35.340
to significantly improve your
video conferencing setup.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.790
And this might be very
much worth the investment,

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:39.990
especially for facing
these types of situations

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:40.930
that are now highlighted.

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:43.740
Now, in one effort to
combine all of these points,

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.860
I think that there's
one common denominator

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:47.210
that really, really defines

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:48.990
whether you are able to prepare yourself

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:50.200
in the right way or not.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:52.960
Because the truth is even
if you're 10 minutes early,

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.730
10 minutes in the room
before the meeting starts,

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:57.630
this will usually barely be enough

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.380
to connect all your devices
to the other devices.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.640
This will be barely
enough preparation time

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:05.850
to make sure everything is
set up and works properly.

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:07.760
But as soon as you have
one little problem,

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:10.630
as soon as one thing does
not work as expected,

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:12.380
usually you will run out of time

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:15.090
and you will not be able to
start the meeting on time.

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:18.150
From my experience, especially
for important meetings

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.020
where you want to make sure
that everything works smoothly,

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:22.450
you should be in the meeting room

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:25.740
at least 30 minutes before the
meeting starts to make sure,

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:27.870
to give you enough time,
to set everything up,

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:29.390
and to have this fallback option

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:31.770
that if something is not working properly,

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.360
that you at least have a chance

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:35.020
to find maybe a replacement device,

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:37.910
to maybe get an additional
microphone from somewhere,

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:39.970
to potentially call a
technical support person

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:41.970
that often many client organizations have.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:43.970
If you are not in the room 30 minutes

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:45.210
before the meeting starts,

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:47.070
you will not be able to do this on time.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:49.280
So as with most other
things in life as well,

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:50.750
good preparation is key.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:53.300
Be sure that you behave as a professional,

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.590
especially if you're working
in professional services.

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:58.100
And a big part of that
certainly is to make sure

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:00.250
that you prepare for
your important meetings

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:02.530
in a very meticulous and diligent way.

